zes actual configuration application and captures public Ready output.\",\n          \"B_elect_transfer\": \"Establishes the initial authority and current-term commit prefix.\"\n        },\n        \"obligation_relation_kind\": \"consumption\",\n        \"counterevidence\": [\n          \"The heartbeat consumer explicitly invokes current r.trk.Voters.VoteResult, whose JointConfig implementation requires both majority configurations.\",\n          \"switchToConfig does not clear the pending acknowledgment set, so preservation does not itself imply a defect.\",\n          \"A later context can release earlier queued requests; restricting this question to a single request is necessary to avoid an unsupported per-request majority assertion.\"\n        ],\n        \"unknowns\": [],\n        \"priority\": 0,\n        \"trigger_rationale\": \"The current-configuration consumer and retained context-keyed support give a concrete distinction between an old-only majority and both joint majorities. Five initial voters transitioning to incoming {1,4,5} and outgoing {1,2,3,4,5} permits actual delayed replies from 2 and 3 to form only the outgoing majority with leader 1; reply 4 then supplies the incoming majority. This avoids fabricated membership state and needs no new process or snapshot bootstrap.\"\n      },\n      \"status\": \"escalated\",\n      \"parent_candidate_id\": null,\n      \"fork_reason\": \"\",\n      \"stop_reason\": \"Fix a necessary local eligibility condition from documented safe-read quorum and joint-majority semantics; do not equate this condition with full client linearizability.\",\n      \"resume_conditions\": [],\n      \"obligation_id\": \"C_read_joint_eligibility\",\n      \"result_claim_ids\": [\n        \"C_read_joint_eligibility\"\n      ],\n      \"open_issue_ids\": [],\n      \"current_applicability\": \"within_recorded_scope\",\n      \"executions\": [\n        {\n          \"check_id\": \"e5a5dd0477a44775af322e051af102a8\",\n          \"artifa